tem in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}54 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}54 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "edda5ea9539e588e535263dd2e2cb91d7e2ef42baf775b9d0202da49fd9a4f30"} {"question" "q-85275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-41780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-7 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=12\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-41780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}14 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 29\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ -19\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{150}[\\begin{aligned}29 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ -19\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-41780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "edda5ea9539e588e535263dd2e2cb91d7e2ef42baf775b9d0202da49fd9a4f30"} {"question" "q-41780" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-41780" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ \\frac{11}{25}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{25} \\\\ \\frac{1}{75}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{19}{25}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-41780" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 44\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 44\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{(-9)^{2}+(-11)^{2}+(-9)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=2\\sqrt{71}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "edda5ea9539e588e535263dd2e2cb91d7e2ef42baf775b9d0202da49fd9a4f30"} {"question" "q-85280" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85280" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{17}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{71}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{7}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{17}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{71}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85280" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{71}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-41783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-41783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{30}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-41783" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "edda5ea9539e588e535263dd2e2cb91d7e2ef42baf775b9d0202da49fd9a4f30"} {"question" "q-41783" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-41783" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{6}"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{7}"] ["math" "5\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{30}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{70}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-41783" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{30}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 7\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+(-7)^{2}}=\\sqrt{114}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "edda5ea9539e588e535263dd2e2cb91d7e2ef42baf775b9d0202da49fd9a4f30"} {"question" "q-85278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{111}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{114}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{11}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{141}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{14}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{114}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85284" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85284" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{110}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85284" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "edda5ea9539e588e535263dd2e2cb91d7e2ef42baf775b9d0202da49fd9a4f30"} {"question" "q-85284" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85284" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{101}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{110}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{111}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{11}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85284" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{110}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "edda5ea9539e588e535263dd2e2cb91d7e2ef42baf775b9d0202da49fd9a4f30"} {"question" "q-85909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "q-85909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/state.json" "file_sha256" "8ccc60d69dd08c311625f4ceecf789c57e1c1ebd348a9f21dac24f4d63379501"} {"question" "e-5589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$, where \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/assets/8b006c23e83405be4cd93396e5bde38c0dbcb5f8bb735b125d0b05f439e5b0d9.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/example-5589.json" "file_sha256" "3f945b9c9bf2e531d05b9ffa98912a46233e82772d66591c89e9d8a4cdd6eee0"} {"question" "e-5589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/assets/6fed2ac5452cf11059550a0c6328914c0e897714ca14a68f71df8eeb6d93471d.png)\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/example-5589.json" "file_sha256" "3f945b9c9bf2e531d05b9ffa98912a46233e82772d66591c89e9d8a4cdd6eee0"} {"question" "e-5588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=12 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-12 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/example-5588.json" "file_sha256" "310ae4dd0436820399a21aef758b261ed92d8d101555a7591d0d8e9440e7e74e"} {"question" "e-5588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/assets/fa49e62c4e6fce14470e1641ef5e9a8c986778d74812e1bac82d3ba9df94aef8.png)\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-24 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-24 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/example-5588.json" "file_sha256" "310ae4dd0436820399a21aef758b261ed92d8d101555a7591d0d8e9440e7e74e"} {"question" "e-7429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $\\Vert A\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert.$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/assets/8200befaaea3a0cdec7e423999839c72d48fc67110fa577c8707e182c4524aff.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/example-7429.json" "file_sha256" "6c44f2bd1c758649abc090089b9e826883a4d188f449f06399dcc5b664349f20"} {"question" "e-7429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First of all, we write the given system in the form $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $\\Vert A\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert$ is attained when $\\mathbf{x}={\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nIn this case, $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $\\Vert A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert$ is the distance between the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ and the column space.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\Vert A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}-\\mathbf{b}\\Vert =\\sqrt{(-12)^2+0^2+(-12)^2+0^2}=12\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071086/example-7429.json" "file_sha256" "6c44f2bd1c758649abc090089b9e826883a4d188f449f06399dcc5b664349f20"}] "usage" {"e-5588" [] "e-7429" [] "q-41783" [] "q-41775" [] "e-5589" []} "basis" 2528 "retractions" [[17592186134288 :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n${x_{1} + 2x_{2} + x_{3} = 18; x_{1} - 3x_{2} = 0; x_{1} + 2x_{2} - x_{3} = 18; x_{1} - x_{2} = 0$"] [17592186134291 :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax = b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax - b‖$.\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186133530 :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$, where\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186133530 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form\n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{T}Ax^(^) &= A^{T}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(^) &= (A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T}b \\\\\n&= (\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133529 :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n${-2x_{1} - x_{2} = 12; x_{1} - 2x_{2} + x_{3} = 0; - x_{2} - 2x_{3} =-12; x_{1} - x_{3} = 0$"] [17592186133529 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form\n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{T}Ax^(^) &= A^{T}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(^) &= (A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T}b \\\\\n&= (\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix})^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-24 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-24 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133586 :question/problem "Consider the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $\\Vert A\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert$.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186133586 :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form\n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(A)\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(b) \\\\\nAx &= b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{T}Ax^(^) &= A^{T}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^(^) &= (A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T}b \\\\\n&= (\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix})^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $\\Vert A\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert$ is attained when\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}={\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}\n$$\n\nwhich is the least-squares solution of the system\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the column space of $A$, and $\\Vert A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert$ is the distance between the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ and the column space.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAx^(^) - b &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\Vert A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}-\\mathbf{b}\\Vert =\\sqrt{(-12)^2+0^2+(-12)^2+0^2}=12\\sqrt{2}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186132171 17592186132172 17592186132173 17592186132174 17592186132175 17592186132189 17592186132190 17592186132191 17592186132192 17592186132193] "immutable_fields" [[17592186132170 :answer-field/correct] [17592186132170 :answer-field/id] [17592186132170 :answer-field/key] [17592186132170 :answer-field/type] [17592186132188 :answer-field/correct] [17592186132188 :answer-field/id] [17592186132188 :answer-fiel